WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:09.360
Healers, priests, hedge witches, shamans,
apothecaries and your nan have known since

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:16.680
a caveman first shoved his hand into a beehive
that bee stings hurt, but now it’s official.

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:19.289
Yes that, and honey is good for you.

00:00:19.289 --> 00:00:25.240
A recent Meta-study by Oxford academics found
that honey is better for treating coughs and

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:29.760
colds - upper respiratory tract infections
- than antibiotics.

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:36.300
And according to a slightly less insightful
study it turns out , bee stings are indeed

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:43.129
painful; the most painful place to be stung
by a bee is on your penis – presuming you

00:00:43.129 --> 00:00:44.129
have one.

00:00:44.129 --> 00:00:49.829
I very much doubt that any woman would have
the balls – or shall we call it curiosity?

00:00:49.829 --> 00:00:51.859
- to give it a go.

00:00:51.859 --> 00:00:57.149
When scientists aren’t throwing bee hives
at naked people to see what happens they’re

00:00:57.149 --> 00:01:01.219
usually seeking approval for some new ‘miracle’
cure.

00:01:01.219 --> 00:01:09.229
And, in 2018, Public Health England recommended
that honey should be prescribed before antibiotics

00:01:09.229 --> 00:01:11.570
for coughs and colds.

00:01:11.570 --> 00:01:19.659
Previous to that in 2007 the FDA approved
Medihoney, a honey-based gel used for wound

00:01:19.659 --> 00:01:20.659
treatment.

00:01:20.659 --> 00:01:25.810
But certain truths are self-evident, and just
as most people know it’s a bad idea to get

00:01:25.810 --> 00:01:31.189
stung on your private parts, humans have known
about the medicinal properties of honey for

00:01:31.189 --> 00:01:38.010
as long as we’ve being sticking our fingers
and other curious parts into holes Thousands

00:01:38.010 --> 00:01:43.759
of years of honey wisdom has been gleaned
through the trial and error process known

00:01:43.759 --> 00:01:49.670
as history – or as I like to call it ‘let’s
see what happens if we rub this on our nipples’.

00:01:49.670 --> 00:01:52.159
And what did our ancestors discover?

00:01:52.159 --> 00:01:55.460
That honey is magic.

00:01:55.460 --> 00:02:01.600
In a world with no antibiotics it was often
the most effective and only cure for any number

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:02.780
of ills.

00:02:02.780 --> 00:02:07.520
And therefore it was highly prized, and sometimes
worshipped.

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:14.250
We have records from the ancient Sumerians
and Egyptians, recipes for salves and other

00:02:14.250 --> 00:02:18.440
remedies, many of which are based on or use
honey.

00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:25.670
Ayurvedic medicine, an ancient Indian form
of healing, uses it to aid everything, from

00:02:25.670 --> 00:02:32.269
vomiting to obesity, stress, jaundice, stomach
ulcers and much more.

00:02:32.269 --> 00:02:37.209
It was often left in tombs for the dead to
take with them to the afterlife, showing how

00:02:37.209 --> 00:02:38.930
highly it was valued.

00:02:38.930 --> 00:02:45.019
There’s even a fifteen thousand year old
cave painting in Spain of a man climbing a

00:02:45.019 --> 00:02:48.440
tree and harvesting honey from a beehive.

00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:53.190
No penises were depicted – or, one hopes,
harmed - in this endeavour.

00:02:53.190 --> 00:02:57.519
The Greeks went even further and worshipped
it as the nectar of the Gods.

00:02:57.519 --> 00:03:01.040
Well, the Greek Gods were always a bit weird.

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:03.950
Cleopatra is reputed to have bathed in honey.

00:03:03.950 --> 00:03:10.510
Some cultures even mellified their dead, which
is the practise of steeping the dead in honey

00:03:10.510 --> 00:03:12.040
to preserve them.

00:03:12.040 --> 00:03:18.290
A 16th century Chinese doctor wrote that the
Arabic people took it one step further.

00:03:18.290 --> 00:03:25.220
In a unique twist on old age social care,
elderly Arabic men sacrificed themselves for

00:03:25.220 --> 00:03:26.799
their descendants.

00:03:26.799 --> 00:03:31.800
As they neared the end of their life they
supposedly gave up food and drink, only consumed

00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:34.079
honey – and bathed in it.

00:03:34.079 --> 00:03:39.459
Then, when death finally came, they were mellified
in stone coffins.

00:03:39.459 --> 00:03:46.299
A hundred years later they were exhumed, their
bodies having decomposed and mixed with the

00:03:46.299 --> 00:03:47.299
honey.

00:03:47.299 --> 00:03:54.739
This corpse-infused honey was then used to
heal wounds, fractures and limbs, with only

00:03:54.739 --> 00:03:57.469
a small amount taken internally for the cure.

00:03:57.469 --> 00:03:59.030
So, that’s alright then.

00:03:59.030 --> 00:04:01.239
Not disturbing in the slightest.

00:04:01.239 --> 00:04:07.000
What is disturbing is the story – though
perhaps a legend – of an archaeologist who

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:15.090
unearthed two sealed earthenware jars full
of Pre-Hellenistic honey at a dig in northern

00:04:15.090 --> 00:04:16.090
Israel.

00:04:16.090 --> 00:04:19.250
He tasted the honey, which was apparently
quite good.

00:04:19.250 --> 00:04:25.280
But then, as he dug a little deeper, what
he discovered made him rather regret his ancient

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:30.910
amuse bouche – in the bottom of the jar,
were the bones of an infant child.

00:04:30.910 --> 00:04:31.910
Sweet.

00:04:31.910 --> 00:04:35.140
That’s rather vomit-inducing if you ask
me.

00:04:35.140 --> 00:04:37.570
Do you know what else is vomit-induced?

00:04:37.570 --> 00:04:38.830
Honey.

00:04:38.830 --> 00:04:44.740
That’s right, honey is little more than
vomit.

00:04:44.740 --> 00:04:46.550
Bee vomit.

00:04:46.550 --> 00:04:52.690
The honey making process starts with scavenger
bees collecting nectar and pollen from flowers,

00:04:52.690 --> 00:04:56.640
then transporting it back to the hive as they
start to digest it.

00:04:56.640 --> 00:05:04.740
Here they regurgitate it, passing it on to
worker bees who repeatedly consume, semi-digest

00:05:04.740 --> 00:05:12.760
and vomit it up again and again and again,
until finally, somehow, they sense it’s

00:05:12.760 --> 00:05:13.760
ready.

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:17.930
That or they’re just sick of the whole process.

00:05:17.930 --> 00:05:24.110
Finally, they fan it with their wings to evaporate
the last of the water, and then top it with

00:05:24.110 --> 00:05:30.950
a layer of beeswax to seal it into the honeycomb
for a nice tasty treat in the winter months.

00:05:30.950 --> 00:05:37.420
Unless, of course, a wandering beekeeper,
scientist, caveman or penis pops by to steal

00:05:37.420 --> 00:05:38.420
it.

00:05:38.420 --> 00:05:44.100
It’s a bizarre and convoluted procedure
involving complicated chemical processes – but

00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:50.060
put simply, honey is the combined vomit of
many different bees.

00:05:50.060 --> 00:05:56.050
In the past stealing honey usually meant killing
the bees and destroying their home – that

00:05:56.050 --> 00:06:04.350
was until 1814, when Ukranaian Petro Prokopovych
invented the first prototype for a wooden

00:06:04.350 --> 00:06:11.260
hive frame and ever since we’ve been able
to humanely farm honey without causing too

00:06:11.260 --> 00:06:12.900
much disruption.

00:06:12.900 --> 00:06:20.710
But it is this mysterious process, powered
by the enzymes in bees’ stomachs that semi-digest

00:06:20.710 --> 00:06:25.610
the nectar, which grants honey its unique
qualities.

00:06:25.610 --> 00:06:33.310
By repeatedly digesting it, and drying it,
over and over, the bees create a supersaturated

00:06:33.310 --> 00:06:39.220
solution that contains more dissolved material
than the solvent - in this case the water

00:06:39.220 --> 00:06:41.540
- can dissolve.

00:06:41.540 --> 00:06:48.970
As a result, honey is not only anti-inflammatory,
antimicrobial and antibacterial but also has

00:06:48.970 --> 00:06:51.180
a variety of other health benefits.

00:06:51.180 --> 00:06:56.240
It’s rich in antioxidants, which are thought
to ward off heart attacks, strokes and even

00:06:56.240 --> 00:06:57.820
some types of cancer.

00:06:57.820 --> 00:07:04.380
It may improve cholesterol levels, it reputedly
reduces ulcers and gastrointestinal disorders,

00:07:04.380 --> 00:07:06.820
and can heal wounds and burns.

00:07:06.820 --> 00:07:12.480
It’s even slightly anti-viral and has proved
effective when applied to cold sores, which

00:07:12.480 --> 00:07:14.850
are caused by the Herpes virus.

00:07:14.850 --> 00:07:22.520
Honey can impede the growth of E.coli, salmonella
and MRSA, the famous antibiotic-resistant

00:07:22.520 --> 00:07:23.840
hospital bug.

00:07:23.840 --> 00:07:28.511
One of the reasons honey is so hard to understand
is that there are many different types, with

00:07:28.511 --> 00:07:35.350
a variety of properties and effects, all dependent
on the type of nectar and pollen used to produce

00:07:35.350 --> 00:07:36.380
the honey.

00:07:36.380 --> 00:07:42.650
Honey can be made from a single flower – unifloral
– or a combination of many different flowers

00:07:42.650 --> 00:07:43.902
– you guessed it, mutifloral.

00:07:43.902 --> 00:07:52.250
It can be raw, straight from the beehive,
or pasteurized, heated and strained to eliminate

00:07:52.250 --> 00:07:55.570
bacterial or fungal contamination.

00:07:55.570 --> 00:08:02.020
Some types of honey are toxic and can kill
you; some can make you fly – or at least

00:08:02.020 --> 00:08:04.430
make you think you can fly.

00:08:04.430 --> 00:08:07.850
It all depends where the bees get their pollen.

00:08:07.850 --> 00:08:13.310
You can have lavender, rosemary, buckwheat
and clover honey, though the most common honey

00:08:13.310 --> 00:08:15.800
is a mix of many pollen.

00:08:15.800 --> 00:08:22.120
As a rule of thumb, dark honey is generally
higher in antibacterial and antioxidant properties

00:08:22.120 --> 00:08:23.330
than the lighter variety.

00:08:23.330 --> 00:08:27.850
One of them, Manuka is one of the most prized
in the world.

00:08:27.850 --> 00:08:34.310
It’s made in New Zealand and top quality
Manuka can sell for six pounds per gram.

00:08:34.310 --> 00:08:41.579
But the prize for most expensive honey goes
to Elvish honey, supremely rare and found

00:08:41.579 --> 00:08:47.130
in a deep Turkish cave, it goes for forty-one
pounds per gram.

00:08:47.130 --> 00:08:52.060
It’s reputed to have extraordinary health
benefits due to the rich mineral content in

00:08:52.060 --> 00:08:56.000
the cave and the medicinal plants in the surrounding
area.

00:08:56.000 --> 00:08:58.899
And the stuff that can make you fly?

00:08:58.899 --> 00:09:03.180
That’s the aptly named Mad Honey.

00:09:03.180 --> 00:09:09.620
Made from the poisonous rhododendron plant
in Turkey and the Himalayas, it has hallucinogenic

00:09:09.620 --> 00:09:12.279
and psychoactive properties.

00:09:12.279 --> 00:09:19.389
Nepalese honey-hunters scale vertical cliff
faces to harvest it, whilst enduring a hundred

00:09:19.389 --> 00:09:20.860
brutal bee stings.

00:09:20.860 --> 00:09:28.680
A Greek army got off their tits on Mad Honey
in 410 BC, when according to Xenophon's Anabasis,

00:09:28.680 --> 00:09:35.069
on an expedition into Persian territory they
came upon a village with lots of beehives

00:09:35.069 --> 00:09:37.029
and gorged on it.

00:09:37.029 --> 00:09:43.070
Some went mad, seemingly intoxicated, others
appeared on the point of death – they all

00:09:43.070 --> 00:09:50.089
fully recovered the following day and presumably
remembered they were there to stab Persians,

00:09:50.089 --> 00:09:51.090
not to get high.

00:09:51.090 --> 00:09:55.560
And the bad news for you honey lovers out
there is that while honey is effective when

00:09:55.560 --> 00:10:01.790
applied externally, its health benefits are
significantly less once digested – your

00:10:01.790 --> 00:10:06.079
stomach acid rapidly denatures most of the
beneficial molecules.

00:10:06.079 --> 00:10:11.670
It certainly won’t cure Covid and since
about 10% of honey contains botulism – the

00:10:11.670 --> 00:10:18.889
deadliest toxin known to man – a teaspoon
of it can kill an infant, but not an adult.

00:10:18.889 --> 00:10:23.250
So then, why did your mother shove a teaspoon
of honey down your throat when you had winter

00:10:23.250 --> 00:10:24.250
sniffles?

00:10:24.250 --> 00:10:29.240
Well, mother knows best and she might have
been on to something.

00:10:29.240 --> 00:10:35.080
Before it hits the stomach, honey coats your
throat and esophagus as it slips down, thus

00:10:35.080 --> 00:10:38.180
treating upper respiratory tract infections.

00:10:38.180 --> 00:10:43.889
Honey has consistently beaten off-the-shelf
cough suppressants and antihistamines to ease

00:10:43.889 --> 00:10:47.430
coughs and sniffles in a number of clinical
studies.

00:10:47.430 --> 00:10:53.380
Honey’s antibacterial properties may prove
to be humanity’s saviour in the decades

00:10:53.380 --> 00:10:54.600
to come.

00:10:54.600 --> 00:11:00.899
Every year antibiotic resistance becomes an
increasingly pressing issue – the WHO estimates

00:11:00.899 --> 00:11:08.140
antibiotic-resistant superbugs could kill
over 10 million per year by 2050.

00:11:08.140 --> 00:11:15.040
A pioneering team at the University of Birmingham
are working on a range of honey-based sprays,

00:11:15.040 --> 00:11:22.189
creams and a powder that will be able to treat
infections and kill bacteria, even those that

00:11:22.189 --> 00:11:25.660
fail to respond to modern antibiotics.

00:11:25.660 --> 00:11:31.370
The powder is particularly exciting, because
when mixed with water it creates a gel that

00:11:31.370 --> 00:11:33.839
can be used in warzones.

00:11:33.839 --> 00:11:41.860
Why is the team especially interested in re-purposing
honey and not developing new antibiotics instead?

00:11:41.860 --> 00:11:49.730
Because humans have been using it to treat
infections for over 8,000 years and, unlike

00:11:49.730 --> 00:11:56.100
modern medicine, bacteria has never been able
to evolve a resistance to it.

00:11:56.100 --> 00:12:02.990
It’s just as effective now as it was when
caveman first smeared it on his sore nipple.

00:12:02.990 --> 00:12:10.180
Furthermore, despite their best efforts, scientists
have been unable to engineer bacteria that

00:12:10.180 --> 00:12:13.139
is resistant to honey in the lab.

00:12:13.139 --> 00:12:17.649
This is because honey doesn’t work like
any other antibiotic.

00:12:17.649 --> 00:12:22.959
As already mentioned, honey is a supersaturated
solution, it has absorbed more sugar than

00:12:22.959 --> 00:12:26.759
should be possible for the volume of water
it contains.

00:12:26.759 --> 00:12:33.430
This means honey is absolutely craving more
water, and what does bacteria contain?

00:12:33.430 --> 00:12:40.850
That’s right, a good dose of water, therefore
when honey contacts a bacterium it rapidly

00:12:40.850 --> 00:12:45.480
sucks all the water out, neutralising it.

00:12:45.480 --> 00:12:53.060
Since this is a mechanical process, bacteria
has never found a way to evolve a natural

00:12:53.060 --> 00:12:59.230
defence against it, and there’s a good chance
it never will.

00:12:59.230 --> 00:13:04.209
Except for the botulism spores we mentioned
earlier which only exist in honey because

00:13:04.209 --> 00:13:09.060
they’re already dried out, so there’s
no water for the honey to extract.

00:13:09.060 --> 00:13:12.709
But pretty much nothing else stands a chance.

00:13:12.709 --> 00:13:19.829
And yes, it’s ability to exterminate bacteria
like a gloopy, sticky dalek is what gives

00:13:19.829 --> 00:13:22.040
honey its eternal shelf-life.

00:13:22.040 --> 00:13:40.109
So then, it turns out honey is magic after
all.

